  "finalContent": "# Grok persistent-session proof — safety boundary\n\nBaton's Phase 11.1 persistent-session safety boundary is defined by contracts PS1–PS8 and an explicit safety gate before live provider proof.\n\nThe coordinator reuses a verified session for follow-up turns only while the handle stays attached and idle; kill, crash, timeout, or unattached replay ends reuse. Follow-up admission commits after `Ack{ok:true}`; refusal restores prior state. Coordinator turn epochs fence stale adapter events. Each accepted turn gets fresh capture, verification, and verdict.\n\nResume and fork are explicit spawn policies with typed pre-allocation failure. Grok `session/load` is carded native; `x.ai/session/fork` stays `planned` until wire schemas are fixture-pinned. `recover()` requires a native handshake matching the persisted `sessionRef` before leaving `orphaned`; PID alone is never trusted.\n\nThe safety gate requires fake-backed driver tests for PS1–PS5 (Claude, Codex, Grok), protocol fixtures for PS6–PS8 before provider probes, and live proof of verified first turn, same-session/PID follow-up, second verification, then kill/reap. Resume/fork probes follow only after persisted references and worktree-coherence checks pass.\n\nThis edit is the second public Baton turn on the same persistent session, exercising PS1 follow-up reuse after the first turn was verified.\n\nturn=2",
